Patch 21: promote only the uneven-4-way partition disable to speed 1.

Standalone. Composes with patch 20 (they set different flags), and is a weaker
alternative to patch 0019.

Speed 2 sets two related flags together:

    sf->part_sf.disable_uneven_4way_partitions = true;
    sf->part_sf.disable_ext_partitions         = true;

Patch 0019 promoted the second to speed 1 and landed at +15.92% / +0.52% on A1,
ratio 30.6 against a bar of 35 -- a large speedup carrying slightly too much
quality cost. This patch promotes the first instead.

The reasoning is that the extended partition family is not homogeneous. The
uneven 4-way shapes (HORZ_4A/4B, VERT_4A/4B) split a block into unequal quarters
and pay off only where content happens to align to that particular division;
the 3-way shapes are the more generally useful members and carry more of the
family's coding gain. Disabling the 4-way subset alone should therefore give up
a smaller share of the BD-rate than of the search time -- which is the only kind
of change that moves a ratio, as opposed to sliding along it.

This is a strictly weaker change than 0019 and will deliver a proportionally
smaller speedup. It earns its own arm because of what either outcome tells us.
If the 4-way subset accounts for a disproportionate share of extended-partition
search time, the arithmetic clears the bar where 0019 could not. If it accounts
for a proportionate share, the ratio lands near 0019's 30.6 and the conclusion
is that the family cannot usefully be split -- worth establishing before more
effort goes into subdividing it, and cheap to establish.

Note this is an assignment move, not a new runtime test: the flag already exists
and is already honoured throughout the partition search.

-DAVM_S1_DISABLE_UNEVEN_4WAY=0 disables the promotion without a patch edit.

